            <ArticleTitle>Successful Outcome of Mediastinitis After 26 Days Delay in Diagnosis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>84-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5505/1304.7361.2014.55798</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Acute mediastinitis, which is characterized by inflammation of thoracic tissues, is a life-threatening infection and a surgical emergency. The mortality rate for this ailment is reported between 14-42%, which makes it important to diagnose and treat as soon as possible. We presented and discussed a case of a 38-year-old male patient with blunt trauma to the chest and back from falling who reported to the emergency department after 26 days with severe chest pain and a toxic condition. He underwent an anterolateral thoracotomy and several rounds of pleural flushing; ultimately, he survived.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
